The statement that creates a database is `CREATE DATABASE`; the database is then selected so that the next statements apply to it:

```sql
CREATE DATABASE SELLS;
USE SELLS;
```

*Remarks.* `USE SELLS;` is the MySQL form (in PostgreSQL the client reconnects with `\c sells`). SQLite has no `CREATE DATABASE`: the database is the file created when it is opened (`sqlite3 SELLS.db`). In a graphical DBMS such as MS Access, the same step is File - New - Blank database, saved under the name SELLS. The mark is for the database being created with the exact name SELLS.

The relations are filled with `INSERT` statements. The paper fixes the numbers of rows (5 to 7 furniture rows, 3 to 4 customer rows) and asks for **consistency**: every `CustomerOrder.CustomerID` must exist in `Customer`, and every `CustomerOrderLine` must have its `OrderID` in `CustomerOrder` and its `FurnitureID` in `Furniture`. For this reason the parent relations (`Customer`, `Furniture`) are filled first, then `CustomerOrder`, then `CustomerOrderLine`. The values respect the field sizes (an ID of at most 6 characters, a name of at most 15, an e-mail address of at most 20) and the item 10765, needed in subtask (iv), is included. The data below are an example; any consistent data are accepted.

```sql
-- Customers (4 rows)
INSERT INTO Customer VALUES ('C00001', 'Nkeng Paul',    'paul.nkeng@mail.cm', 677123456);
INSERT INTO Customer VALUES ('C00002', 'Achu Beatrice', 'achu.b@mail.cm',     699456789);
INSERT INTO Customer VALUES ('C00003', 'Tabi Samuel',   'tabi.s@mail.cm',     655987654);
INSERT INTO Customer VALUES ('C00004', 'Mbua Grace',    'mbua.grace@mail.cm', 670321987);

-- Furniture (6 rows)
INSERT INTO Furniture VALUES (10765, 'Sofa 3 seats', 'Living room', 185000.00, 'Mobilux');
INSERT INTO Furniture VALUES (10766, 'Armchair',     'Living room',  72500.00, 'Mobilux');
INSERT INTO Furniture VALUES (20311, 'Dining table', 'Dining room', 120000.00, 'Bois&Co');
INSERT INTO Furniture VALUES (20312, 'Dining chair', 'Dining room',  18000.00, 'Bois&Co');
INSERT INTO Furniture VALUES (30458, 'Double bed',   'Bedroom',     210000.00, 'Dormir');
INSERT INTO Furniture VALUES (40102, 'Writing desk', 'Office',       65000.00, 'Dormir');

-- Orders (5 rows): each order belongs to an existing customer
INSERT INTO CustomerOrder VALUES (5001, 'C00001', '2022-05-03');
INSERT INTO CustomerOrder VALUES (5002, 'C00002', '2022-05-10');
INSERT INTO CustomerOrder VALUES (5003, 'C00003', '2022-05-17');
INSERT INTO CustomerOrder VALUES (5004, 'C00001', '2022-06-01');
INSERT INTO CustomerOrder VALUES (5005, 'C00004', '2022-06-08');

-- Order lines (9 rows): each line belongs to an existing order and an existing item
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5001, 10765, 1);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5001, 10766, 2);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5002, 20311, 1);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5002, 20312, 6);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5003, 10765, 1);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5004, 10765, 1);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5004, 40102, 1);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5005, 30458, 1);
INSERT INTO CustomerOrderLine VALUES (5005, 10765, 2);
```

Reading the transactions back: customer C00001 (Nkeng Paul) placed two orders (5001 for one sofa 10765 and two armchairs 10766, 5004 for a sofa and a desk), C00002 (Achu Beatrice) one order for a dining table and six chairs, C00003 (Tabi Samuel) one order for a sofa, and C00004 (Mbua Grace) one order for a bed and two sofas. Four customers, six items, five orders and nine order lines are stored. The statements were run in SQLite with foreign keys enforced: no insertion was refused, so the data are consistent.

*Marking (8 marks; the paper gives no split):* 5 to 7 furniture rows and 3 to 4 customer rows entered correctly; orders that refer to existing customers; order lines that refer to existing orders and furniture, with quantities.
